2026-06-06 18:57:23 - INFO - Generating definition summary | metadata={'content': '-  \n- Sputum smear microscopy  \n-  \n- Chest x-ray\n- Sputum culture and drug sensitivity testing(DST) for diagnosis of Drug Resistant TB.\n- Newer rapid diagnostic tools for detection of multi drug resistant (MDR) TB.  \n-  \n- Newer tools under evaluation for diagnosis of MDR/XDR TB  \n-  \n-  \nFigure 6. Algorithm for diagnosis of pulmonary tuberculosis  \n<!-- image -->  \nThis image displays a clinical decision tree used to diagnose and manage persistent coughs (lasting 6 weeks or more). It begins with two sputum smear examinations: if one or both are positive, treatment for tuberculosis (TB) is initiated; if both are negative, the patient receives antibiotics for 10�14 days. If the cough persists after antibiotic therapy, further testing � including repeat sputum smears and chest X-ray � is performed. Based on results, patients may be treated for TB or managed as having non-TB causes (e.g., post-viral cough). The flowchart guides clinicians through differential diagnosis based on microbiological and radiological findings.  \n- Paediatric TB patients (Figure 7) -In  all  children  with  presumptive  intra-thoracic  TB,  microbiological  confirmation should  be  sought  through  examination  of  respiratory  specimens  (like  sputum by expectoration, gastric aspirate, induced sputum, bronchio-alveolar lavage or any other appropriate specimen) with diagnostic test like smear microscopy or rapid molecular test or culture.\n- -For children with presumptive extra-pulmonary TB, appropriate specimens from the presumed sites of involvement should be obtained for microscopy, culture and DST, rapid molecular test and histopathological examination.\n- -In  the  event  of  negative  or  unavailable  microbiological  results, a  diagnosis  of probable TB in children should be based on the presence of abnormalities consistent with TB on radiography, a history of exposure to an infectious case, evidence of TB infection and clinical findings suggestive of TB.  \nFigure 7. Diagnostic algorithm for pediatric pulmonary tuberculosis  \n<!-- image -->  \nThis image displays a clinical decision tree or algorithm for diagnosing and managing suspected pulmonary tuberculosis (TB) in children, likely from a pediatric context given the reference to �pediatricians.� It begins with identifying symptoms such as fever, cough lasting \u22652 weeks, weight loss/gain, and exposure history. The flowchart guides clinicians through steps including sputum examination, antibiotic trial if negative initially, repeat testing, chest X-ray/monotest if still negative after two rounds, and finally initiating TB treatment or referring to a pediatrician based on diagnostic results � whether suggestive of TB or non-TB.  \nThe diagram is designed to help healthcare providers systematically evaluate and act upon suspected TB cases in children using available tools like sputum analysis and imaging, while accounting for false negatives or atypical presentations common in pediatric populations.  \n- Extra-pulmonary TB -Demonstration of AFB bacilli in a smear from extra pulmonary site is often difficult because of low bacillary load. The clinical features pertaining to the system affected should be considered in the diagnosis of extra pulmonary tuberculosis.\n- -However, the following are some of the special investigations which are helpful in diagnosing  extra  pulmonary  tuberculosis.  These  may  be  radiological,  cytological  / pathological, biochemical and immunological (Figure 8).\n- (a) Fine Needle Aspirations Cytology and direct smear examination\n- (b) Excision / Biopsy of specimen for histopathological examination\n- (c) Fluid for cytology , biochemical analysis and smear examination\n- (d) X-ray of the involved region\n- (e) Ultrasonography for abdominal tuberculosis\n- (f) Culture for Mycobacterium tuberculosis\n- (g) Computerized tomography (CT) and  magnetic resonance imaging (MRI)  \nFigure 8. Diagnostic algorithm for TB lymphadenitis  \n<!-- image -->  \nThis flowchart outlines the diagnostic and management steps for suspected tuberculosis (TB) lymphadenitis:  \n1. It begins with clinical criteria � lymph node enlargement \u22652 cm, possibly with abscess or discharge, plus evidence of TB elsewhere.\n2. A 2-week trial of anti-TB antibiotics is initiated empirically.\n3. If nodes persist after treatment, TB lymphadenitis is suspected, and pus from the node is sent for Fine Needle Aspiration Cytology (FNAC).\n4. Diagnosis is confirmed if FNAC shows acid-fast bacilli (AFB) on ZN smear or histopathological changes suggestive of TB.\n5. If FNAC results are inconclusive, excisional biopsy may be performed before starting treatment.  \nThe chart emphasizes a stepwise approach: empirical therapy first, then diagnostic confirmation via cytology or biopsy if needed � especially important in children under 14 years where FNAC is preferred over biopsy when possible.  \n- Diagnosis of Pleural TB -Tubercular pleural  effusion  is  considered as  extra  pulmonary  tuberculosis.  TB Patients  presenting  with  chest  pain  with  or  without  difficulty  in  breathing  for  more than two weeks should be referred for chest radiograph.\n- -Biochemical  and  cytological  analysis  of  the  aspirated  pleura  fluid  will  help  in confirming the diagnosis. Pleural fluid is generally an exudate with mainly lymphocytes and few mesothelial cells. -Pleural biopsy is confirmatory in a high proportion of patients.\n- -In  pleural  effusion,  chest  X-ray  features  may  include  obliteration  of  costophrenic angle and varying degree of effusion.', 'disease_category': 'COMMON CONDITIONS', 'disease_name': 'TUBERCULOSIS'}
